1
00:00:04,644 --> 00:00:17,130
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you were saying earlier, what is interesting was that a person cannot get the right view without first abandoning the value of sensuality.

2
00:00:18,270 --> 00:00:20,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

3
00:00:21,031 --> 00:00:28,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Abandoning the value of sensuality is not necessarily like abandoning sensuality, is it?

4
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:31,141
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that, that, that is what abandoning sensuality is.

5
00:00:31,161 --> 00:00:32,081
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the root of it.

6
00:00:32,101 --> 00:00:38,624
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, like, not performing sensual acts is not necessarily abandoning sensuality.

7
00:00:38,924 --> 00:00:44,526
[SPEAKER_01]: You might be abstained from engaging with sensuality, like sexuality and so on.

8
00:00:44,546 --> 00:00:54,210
[SPEAKER_01]: You might be restrained there and celibate, but you are not abandoning the value of sense pleasure, which means you're not free from sensuality, even if you're physically withdrawn from it, as the Buddha would say.

9
00:00:55,300 --> 00:01:02,184
[SPEAKER_01]: But in those suttas we quoted in the past, where the Buddha would establish lay people or anybody else in the right view, that would be the thing.

10
00:01:02,664 --> 00:01:11,449
[SPEAKER_01]: First, generosity, talk about the future life, goodness, and so on and so on, and then the Buddha would start talking and teaching them about the perils of sense desires.

11
00:01:12,550 --> 00:01:14,551
[SPEAKER_01]: How little satisfaction they provide.

12
00:01:14,571 --> 00:01:17,553
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the nature, sensuality, he would teach about that.

13
00:01:18,494 --> 00:01:22,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, in its nature, sense desires are not worthy.

14
00:01:22,558 --> 00:01:29,965
[SPEAKER_01]: In its nature, sense desires of any kind, engaging with sense pleasures and seeking them out, is unwholesome.

15
00:01:30,666 --> 00:01:33,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Regardless of what extent, and this is acceptable.

16
00:01:33,308 --> 00:01:33,508
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

17
00:01:33,929 --> 00:01:39,234
[SPEAKER_01]: In its nature, anything that pertains to the nature of sense desire, must be abandoned.

18
00:01:43,298 --> 00:01:45,841
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the right view would arise in those people.

19
00:01:45,881 --> 00:01:48,744
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you would teach them this is suffering, this is the origin of suffering and so on.

20
00:01:49,485 --> 00:02:01,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Because for as long as you don't abandon the value of sense pleasures, you're preventing the necessary basis for the right view to be established.

21
00:02:03,275 --> 00:02:08,837
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if those people later on returned to sensuality, they never returned to the value of sensuality.

22
00:02:09,678 --> 00:02:16,141
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they might have returned to sensuality if, you know, they had duties and circumstances taking them back and so on, but they never valued it again.

23
00:02:16,341 --> 00:02:24,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, every time you're back in sensuality, they would have felt ashamed, uncomfortable and unpleasant.

24
00:02:24,424 --> 00:02:26,165
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they were still engaging with sense pleasures,

25
00:02:27,407 --> 00:02:33,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Despite having the right view and so on, but that's because they were satisfied with the right view and they had many things to do, as they would say to the Buddha.

26
00:02:33,672 --> 00:02:36,034
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I have to, excuse me now, you know, I have many things to do.

27
00:02:36,735 --> 00:02:38,216
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha would say, well, do as you see fit.

28
00:02:39,417 --> 00:02:46,903
[SPEAKER_01]: But more often than not, actually, in the suttas, people, upon developing the right view, would ask to go forth.

29
00:02:47,943 --> 00:02:54,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Because life that's rooted and revolves around sensual domain is meaningless.

30
00:02:55,169 --> 00:02:59,232
[SPEAKER_01]: It always was meaningless, but now that whole thing became even more apparent.

31
00:03:01,033 --> 00:03:09,580
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, people would return to it when, you know, they had big responsibilities, people's lives depending on them and so on, then they couldn't just ignore it.

32
00:03:10,941 --> 00:03:17,283
[SPEAKER_01]: But even then, there were a number of cases who returned and then continued practicing celibacy and looking after their wives because they made the promise they will.

33
00:03:17,883 --> 00:03:21,344
[SPEAKER_01]: But they said, it's unfitting for me now to engage in a sexual intercourse with you.

34
00:03:22,425 --> 00:03:22,805
[SPEAKER_01]: And so on.

35
00:03:24,845 --> 00:03:26,246
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is... what is the value?

36
00:03:26,586 --> 00:03:26,886
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

37
00:03:26,906 --> 00:03:28,847
[SPEAKER_01]: The value of pleasure that you get through your senses.

38
00:03:29,647 --> 00:03:31,968
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the sense objects are not a problem.

39
00:03:32,028 --> 00:03:33,268
[SPEAKER_01]: The senses are not a problem.

40
00:03:34,408 --> 00:03:39,330
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the pleasures that arise on account of sense objects and senses are not the problem.

41
00:03:40,397 --> 00:03:47,082
[SPEAKER_01]: You valuing them, desiring them, entertaining them, protecting them, delighting in them, that's the problem.

42
00:03:51,105 --> 00:04:03,493
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to abandon the value of pleasure, which means you have to be willing to accept in that universal manner, that for the rest of your life, sense pleasure is bad not to be engaged with.

43
00:04:05,915 --> 00:04:08,397
[SPEAKER_01]: If you, as I said before, if you fail to do it,

44
00:04:09,551 --> 00:04:26,657
[SPEAKER_01]: You might fail, like, in your acts, as in you might, sort of, go back to sensuality, but you will not fail in establishing the value that sensuality is bad, which means whenever you fail in sustaining such acts of renunciation, you will feel guilty, you will feel ashamed.

45
00:04:27,297 --> 00:04:28,118
[SPEAKER_01]: So, next time...

46
00:04:29,132 --> 00:04:30,473
[SPEAKER_01]: They will protect you from doing it.

47
00:04:31,193 --> 00:04:35,976
[SPEAKER_01]: But, they will not protect you from doing it if you never question the value of sense pleasures.

48
00:04:36,737 --> 00:04:40,759
[SPEAKER_01]: If you basically, even before questioning it, you start defending it and justifying it.

49
00:04:41,900 --> 00:04:45,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, you're never gonna uproot it, even if you live celibate for the rest of your life.

50
00:04:45,910 --> 00:04:50,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you uproot sensuality by uprooting the value of pleasure, not by abstaining physically from it.

51
00:04:50,834 --> 00:04:56,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, abstaining physically from it is a necessary basis for you to see the value of it, and then you can uproot it.

52
00:04:57,158 --> 00:04:59,480
[SPEAKER_01]: But in itself, it will not automatically result in it.

53
00:05:04,083 --> 00:05:12,930
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the value of... of... Basically, it's like learning to see that... By the way, all the sense pleasures are addictive.

54
00:05:13,943 --> 00:05:17,568
[SPEAKER_01]: The definition of addiction means definition of sense domain, sensual domain.

55
00:05:18,810 --> 00:05:22,936
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all these different addictions share that same nature in themselves.

56
00:05:24,839 --> 00:05:25,420
[SPEAKER_01]: They're sensual.

57
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:26,922
[SPEAKER_01]: They provide sense pleasure.

58
00:05:28,847 --> 00:05:35,250
[SPEAKER_01]: So, obviously, sexuality and that kind of deeply rooted central needs are the greatest of all addictions.

59
00:05:35,610 --> 00:05:39,852
[SPEAKER_01]: But as any other addiction, if you abstain from it, it will become more apparent for what it is.

60
00:05:40,572 --> 00:05:43,794
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason you're not seeing your addiction is because you refuse to give it up.

61
00:05:44,994 --> 00:05:49,896
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you start having reasons for giving it up, then you get to see how really addicted you are.

62
00:05:50,497 --> 00:05:54,158
[SPEAKER_01]: You're never going to see how addicted to cigarettes or heroin you are until you try and quit it.

63
00:05:56,279 --> 00:05:57,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you feel all the weight of it.

64
00:06:00,943 --> 00:06:05,506
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the necessary step for giving up addiction is to give it up.

65
00:06:06,827 --> 00:06:07,367
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop doing it.

66
00:06:09,289 --> 00:06:11,030
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an act of will.

67
00:06:11,110 --> 00:06:16,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Act of will to abstain yourself physically from engaging with it, and then the value of it will become more apparent.

68
00:06:17,614 --> 00:06:23,998
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you have to accept pure, unadulterated pain of withdrawal.

69
00:06:24,919 --> 00:06:26,220
[SPEAKER_01]: That has to be the first step.

70
00:06:27,841 --> 00:06:29,222
[SPEAKER_01]: And not just accept it for a week,

71
00:06:30,996 --> 00:06:33,319
[SPEAKER_01]: For ten days, you have to accept it universally.

72
00:06:34,401 --> 00:06:35,682
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know how long it will last.

73
00:06:36,363 --> 00:06:37,004
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter.

74
00:06:37,224 --> 00:06:44,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality will never be valuable, even as something that could free you from that pain, which is kind of the inherent value of sensuality.

75
00:06:44,953 --> 00:06:49,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just it provides you with pleasure, it provides you the only form of escape from pain that you know, as the Buddha said.

76
00:06:50,155 --> 00:07:00,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, even if you don't value sensual pleasures, like, you don't value objects of your senses, you value sensuality as the means of escape from discomfort.

77
00:07:00,559 --> 00:07:01,760
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's already a wrong view.

78
00:07:08,423 --> 00:07:09,343
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already a problem.

79
00:07:14,102 --> 00:07:42,172
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you accept then the value, or rather, if you see through the apparent value of sensuality, and see it as non-valuable, as something to not be delighted, something dangerous, something not worthy, all those descriptions the Buddha would talk about, and you have to accept then the pain that will come out of it, you kind of know it will not gonna last for the rest of your life, but see, hoping that it doesn't last for the rest of your life, means implicitly hoping that you can return back to sensuality.

80
00:07:43,155 --> 00:07:47,497
[SPEAKER_01]: And you must never accept sensuality, even in the utmost remote future.

81
00:07:48,457 --> 00:08:02,043
[SPEAKER_01]: The nature of sensuality is always perilous, always not worthy lighting in, always dangerous, which means, in this life and lives to come, any future, there will never be crossing between the domain of me wanting sensuality and being free from sensuality.

82
00:08:05,344 --> 00:08:07,845
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you'll see, the pain might not even last that long.

83
00:08:10,106 --> 00:08:12,247
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have to accept it as if it's gonna last forever.

84
00:08:15,248 --> 00:08:21,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you don't, to that extent, you're still valuing sensuality as freedom from pain.

85
00:08:26,014 --> 00:08:29,116
[SPEAKER_01]: And all those passages we quoted in the past, that's exactly what he describes.

86
00:08:29,817 --> 00:08:34,179
[SPEAKER_01]: He will make people understand the danger of the sense desires.

87
00:08:34,199 --> 00:08:36,921
[SPEAKER_01]: Not this sense's desire, or half of that sense's desire.

88
00:08:37,661 --> 00:08:43,945
[SPEAKER_01]: In its nature, the act of sense... chasing sense pleasure, going after sense... valuing sense pleasure, that's already why it's dangerous.

89
00:08:45,167 --> 00:08:47,508
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you make people understand that.

90
00:08:47,549 --> 00:08:58,756
[SPEAKER_01]: So, fully understanding it, abandoning it at that universal level, even if you physically remain still attracted and can lapse due to carelessness and go back to centrality, you'll never go back to the value of centrality.

91
00:08:59,956 --> 00:09:01,317
[SPEAKER_01]: That would then dictate your whole life.

92
00:09:02,818 --> 00:09:06,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is the case with most of the people.

93
00:09:10,403 --> 00:09:11,924
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you've seen the extent

94
00:09:14,826 --> 00:09:24,173
[SPEAKER_01]: The extent of gratification is the extent of what's valuable in sensuality for you.

95
00:09:24,213 --> 00:09:26,074
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the extent of its gratification.

96
00:09:26,855 --> 00:09:29,097
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to try and find out what's...

97
00:09:30,058 --> 00:09:32,800
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you have to understand why you are attracted to sensuality.

98
00:09:32,860 --> 00:09:34,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in a psychologizing manner.

99
00:09:34,721 --> 00:09:36,141
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because my hormones... No, no, no.

100
00:09:36,922 --> 00:09:41,144
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you value, mentally, pleasures of the senses?

101
00:09:41,904 --> 00:09:43,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Why can you just not value them?

102
00:09:43,865 --> 00:09:45,406
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because you never try to not value them.

103
00:09:45,947 --> 00:09:48,388
[SPEAKER_01]: You're always taking them for granted and valuing them for what they are.

104
00:09:48,728 --> 00:09:50,409
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure is always regarded as good.

105
00:09:51,370 --> 00:09:52,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure of senses.

106
00:09:52,610 --> 00:09:55,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Only when it gets a bit excessive, you can say, okay, now it's a bit much.

107
00:09:55,452 --> 00:09:55,692
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

108
00:09:56,973 --> 00:10:01,637
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want your value to depend on the quantity or even quality of the pleasure.

109
00:10:02,198 --> 00:10:06,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter if it's lofty, as the Buddha said, if it's heavenly, if it's lowly, like animal realm.

110
00:10:07,163 --> 00:10:09,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter if it's little, if it's a lot.

111
00:10:10,045 --> 00:10:14,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, little bit of excrement smells as bad as the full truckload of it.

112
00:10:15,611 --> 00:10:16,552
[SPEAKER_01]: Same with sensuality.

113
00:10:16,612 --> 00:10:22,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Little bit of it shares the same nature of perilous, danger, not worth adhering to and engaging with.

114
00:10:23,211 --> 00:10:24,232
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter how refined.

115
00:10:24,272 --> 00:10:27,914
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's in that sutta the perfect types of celibacy that Buddha talks about.

116
00:10:27,974 --> 00:10:29,755
[SPEAKER_01]: First is, you don't have sexual intercourse.

117
00:10:29,775 --> 00:10:32,157
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, second, you don't touch women.

118
00:10:32,237 --> 00:10:33,198
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't let them touch you.

119
00:10:33,718 --> 00:10:36,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you don't talk to them.

120
00:10:36,520 --> 00:10:37,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you don't go and listen to them.

121
00:10:38,641 --> 00:10:39,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you don't think about them.

122
00:10:39,862 --> 00:10:45,426
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you don't think about the pleasure of heavens and so on that you will get through such perfect restraint.

123
00:10:46,258 --> 00:10:53,239
[SPEAKER_01]: If you withdraw yourself from all this wanting of this pleasure, that's the perfect celibacy, perfect brahmacharya, as the Buddha described it.

124
00:10:54,600 --> 00:11:00,201
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the only course left for you is to uproot your addiction to pleasure of any kind.

125
00:11:06,402 --> 00:11:13,763
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is no way you would be able to re-evaluate the value of sensuality, unless you're first physically withdrawn from it.

126
00:11:15,010 --> 00:11:17,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless you take on the sensory strength and the precepts.

127
00:11:20,013 --> 00:11:21,873
[SPEAKER_01]: And really, we are talking about eight precepts.

128
00:11:22,734 --> 00:11:24,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Five precepts is how you should live your life.

129
00:11:25,235 --> 00:11:26,716
[SPEAKER_01]: They would separate you from an animal.

130
00:11:28,317 --> 00:11:32,339
[SPEAKER_01]: But five precepts is not a basis for understanding.

131
00:11:32,379 --> 00:11:36,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Five precepts is the lowest minimum threshold of a wholesome life.

132
00:11:36,961 --> 00:11:40,943
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't mean you can get wisdom on account of the minimum threshold of a wholesome life.

133
00:11:46,529 --> 00:11:49,911
[SPEAKER_01]: But yes, if you haven't kept any precepts whatsoever, yeah, five is a good place to start.

134
00:11:50,731 --> 00:12:07,659
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the value in sensuality is that it provides a gratification, a pleasure of some kind, of distracting myself from the fact that I am not satisfied.

135
00:12:08,259 --> 00:12:13,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, value of sensuality is that it provides you a pleasure from the pain of itself.

136
00:12:15,338 --> 00:12:20,339
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality touches you with pain, but at the same time, it offers you solution for that pain.

137
00:12:21,219 --> 00:12:23,399
[SPEAKER_01]: But... like, like racketeering.

138
00:12:23,980 --> 00:12:26,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Hey, if you pay me, I'll make your problems go away.

139
00:12:26,600 --> 00:12:28,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Problems that I put on you, so that you will pay me.

140
00:12:29,480 --> 00:12:30,161
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's a good example.

141
00:12:30,421 --> 00:12:30,521
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

142
00:12:30,941 --> 00:12:34,761
[SPEAKER_01]: So you get racketeered by your own sensuality, your own desires.

143
00:12:35,962 --> 00:12:39,362
[SPEAKER_01]: And the more you pay, the more they're gonna come knocking, because they say, oh, he's susceptible.

144
00:12:39,802 --> 00:12:41,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's push him, let's bleed him dry.

145
00:12:41,663 --> 00:12:42,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

146
00:12:42,063 --> 00:12:43,523
[SPEAKER_01]: It's exactly what sensuality does to you.

147
00:12:44,633 --> 00:12:48,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Until you draw a line for yourself, and then endure the pain.

148
00:12:52,337 --> 00:12:54,038
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're not making it worse, that's the thing.

149
00:12:54,903 --> 00:12:56,304
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing people fear.

150
00:12:57,725 --> 00:13:08,612
[SPEAKER_00]: If I stop going after these pleasures, sexual type pleasures, somehow something bad's going to happen, something really bad.

151
00:13:08,672 --> 00:13:11,194
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because you're not just giving up this pleasure or that pleasure.

152
00:13:11,634 --> 00:13:19,279
[SPEAKER_01]: By giving up value of sensuality, you're giving up the value of the entire world of sensuality, which means... and that's the nature of any addiction.

153
00:13:20,128 --> 00:13:23,709
[SPEAKER_01]: The reason why addictions are hard to give up, you're not giving them up specifically.

154
00:13:23,789 --> 00:13:26,490
[SPEAKER_01]: You're giving up the whole world you lived through them.

155
00:13:27,330 --> 00:13:30,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether it's alcohol, cigarettes, drugs, doesn't matter.

156
00:13:31,191 --> 00:13:38,312
[SPEAKER_01]: The reason they're pleasant is because you get to experience the whole world on the basis of that substance or that addiction or that pleasure.

157
00:13:42,834 --> 00:13:44,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Heroin is not pleasant in itself.

158
00:13:46,193 --> 00:13:50,456
[SPEAKER_01]: Experiencing the world through harrowing is where that pleasure comes from.

159
00:13:51,377 --> 00:13:59,883
[SPEAKER_01]: That peace, undisturbed, the greatest, whatever it is that people would be addicted to, it's defined by the experience of the world on account of it.

160
00:14:00,344 --> 00:14:04,547
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you literally, basically, lose any and complete interest in the world.

161
00:14:05,648 --> 00:14:09,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you stop feeling anything in regard to it, which is what we all want to do from the start.

162
00:14:09,391 --> 00:14:10,211
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody wants peace.

163
00:14:10,792 --> 00:14:12,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody wants non-disturbance.

164
00:14:13,136 --> 00:14:18,800
[SPEAKER_01]: And now you found a substance that provides you with utmost pleasure, whereby nothing can disturb you for as long as the substance lasts.

165
00:14:19,720 --> 00:14:24,884
[SPEAKER_01]: But you only get... that non-disturbance gets defined by the disturbance that doesn't disturb you anymore.

166
00:14:29,547 --> 00:14:32,269
[SPEAKER_01]: And when I say the world defines it, I don't mean you need to go and seek it out.

167
00:14:32,289 --> 00:14:33,550
[SPEAKER_01]: Your senses are your world.

168
00:14:34,070 --> 00:14:35,971
[SPEAKER_01]: So your senses stop disturbing you.

169
00:14:37,072 --> 00:14:38,653
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're high on drugs, for example.

170
00:14:41,517 --> 00:14:46,160
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it gets so painful, the withdrawal from that sense pleasure.

171
00:14:46,180 --> 00:14:48,362
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because you're withdrawing from the only world you know.

172
00:14:48,522 --> 00:14:51,044
[SPEAKER_01]: So it feels like dying, more often than not.

173
00:14:51,124 --> 00:14:52,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Same with cigarettes, by the way.

174
00:14:54,286 --> 00:14:59,050
[SPEAKER_01]: You smoke a cigarette, you're literally inhaling the whole world through them, as Sartre described in his book.

175
00:14:59,550 --> 00:15:00,591
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's absolutely true.

176
00:15:01,212 --> 00:15:06,996
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything, everything that you've been doing, you've been doing on the basis of possibility of that pleasure, enduring of that pleasure.

177
00:15:07,016 --> 00:15:07,816
[SPEAKER_01]: And imagine now,

178
00:15:08,810 --> 00:15:14,795
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to also uproot sexual pleasure, which is far more rooted than any heroin or drug you can be addicted to.

179
00:15:15,836 --> 00:15:18,318
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you will need to have a pretty strong reason for that.

180
00:15:19,079 --> 00:15:20,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, there's no way you'll succeed.

181
00:15:21,221 --> 00:15:26,846
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean, like, even if you don't succeed in doing it, you must not dilute the reasons for doing it.

182
00:15:27,707 --> 00:15:30,449
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you do that, that, for sure, you will not succeed.

183
00:15:33,076 --> 00:15:39,840
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start devaluing the non-value of sensuality, means you're automatically valuing sensuality.

184
00:15:40,340 --> 00:15:51,067
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you say, no, no, no, I don't need to be restrained and I can practice dhamma at the same time, means I don't need to basically abandon the value of sensuality and at the same time I can practice dhamma.

185
00:15:51,087 --> 00:15:51,507
[SPEAKER_01]: What is dhamma?

186
00:15:51,527 --> 00:15:53,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, dhamma is the dispassion and freedom from sensuality.

187
00:15:54,659 --> 00:16:00,144
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're saying you cannot devalue centrality, and by not devaluing it, you are devaluing it.

188
00:16:00,804 --> 00:16:03,226
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a plain, utter, direct contradiction.

189
00:16:03,987 --> 00:16:09,772
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what the Buddha said even to people who are not necessarily following his teachings, when they ask him what criteria should they use for good or bad.

190
00:16:10,112 --> 00:16:16,638
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, whatever practice leads to dispassion, disenchantment, abandoning, renunciation, you should follow, you should value it.

191
00:16:16,738 --> 00:16:17,519
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more valuable.

192
00:16:17,859 --> 00:16:18,479
[SPEAKER_01]: You should do it.

193
00:16:19,380 --> 00:16:19,660
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what?

194
00:16:20,401 --> 00:16:22,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Celibacy or non-celibacy?

195
00:16:23,974 --> 00:16:25,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Which one leads to dispassion?

196
00:16:28,136 --> 00:16:29,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensory strain or non-sensory strain?

197
00:16:30,077 --> 00:16:31,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Which one leads to disenchantment?

198
00:16:32,678 --> 00:16:34,779
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the answer is straightforward.

199
00:16:34,799 --> 00:16:36,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you can say, yeah, but it's too much for me.

200
00:16:36,720 --> 00:16:37,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, that's fine.

201
00:16:38,441 --> 00:16:38,921
[SPEAKER_01]: You're too weak.

202
00:16:38,961 --> 00:16:43,643
[SPEAKER_01]: You admit you're too weak and that's the necessary basis for developing strength, recognizing your weakness.

203
00:16:44,184 --> 00:16:47,025
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you say, no, no, no, I don't need to be sensory strained, that's it.

204
00:16:47,725 --> 00:16:49,146
[SPEAKER_01]: You just throw away the whole thing now.

205
00:16:50,406 --> 00:16:51,067
[SPEAKER_01]: And you stay weak.

206
00:16:51,916 --> 00:16:54,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Same, like people say, I can give up smoking whenever I want.

207
00:16:54,898 --> 00:16:58,361
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't want to give up smoking whenever you want.

208
00:16:59,541 --> 00:17:04,525
[SPEAKER_01]: And without wanting to give up smoking, without removing the value of smoking, you will never give it up.

209
00:17:08,107 --> 00:17:10,429
[SPEAKER_00]: I can stop smoking when I want to stop.

210
00:17:10,469 --> 00:17:12,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but I can't want to stop, basically.

211
00:17:12,330 --> 00:17:13,411
[SPEAKER_00]: But I can't want to stop.

212
00:17:13,871 --> 00:17:16,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, sensuality, I can be restrained, yeah, but you can't want to be restrained.

213
00:17:17,034 --> 00:17:20,196
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you can, you will take it on in that universal manner.

214
00:17:21,427 --> 00:17:23,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality is never worth engaging with.

215
00:17:25,250 --> 00:17:27,751
[SPEAKER_00]: I know it's bad, but I don't...

216
00:17:27,771 --> 00:17:28,512
[SPEAKER_00]: I still think it's...

217
00:17:28,752 --> 00:17:29,512
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not that bad.

218
00:17:29,532 --> 00:17:30,233
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's not that bad.

219
00:17:30,253 --> 00:17:30,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

220
00:17:30,793 --> 00:17:34,856
[SPEAKER_01]: I know it's not as valuable as it presents itself, but it's not that unvaluable either.

221
00:17:34,976 --> 00:17:36,617
[SPEAKER_00]: I've got nothing else to replace it with.

222
00:17:36,637 --> 00:17:37,858
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not seeing the danger of it.

223
00:17:38,738 --> 00:17:39,679
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not seeing the peril.

224
00:17:40,199 --> 00:17:41,620
[SPEAKER_00]: But I need something to replace.

225
00:17:43,372 --> 00:17:48,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, which means I need something to replace the vacuum left by removing the content of that which I value.

226
00:17:48,834 --> 00:17:51,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you never devalue it, you just replace it.

227
00:17:51,215 --> 00:17:52,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Replace one addiction with the other.

228
00:17:53,216 --> 00:17:53,916
[SPEAKER_01]: You're still addicted.

229
00:17:54,316 --> 00:17:54,556
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

230
00:17:59,418 --> 00:18:04,180
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, maybe if I replace my addiction with a better addiction... Refine, refine my addiction.

231
00:18:04,560 --> 00:18:07,001
[SPEAKER_00]: So instead of drugs, I turn to God.

232
00:18:07,241 --> 00:18:08,141
[SPEAKER_01]: See, that's fine.

233
00:18:09,282 --> 00:18:12,143
[SPEAKER_01]: As long as you don't ignore the value of your addiction.

234
00:18:14,599 --> 00:18:19,684
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, yes, let me replace my non-precept keeping with five precepts.

235
00:18:19,724 --> 00:18:20,365
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still better.

236
00:18:21,426 --> 00:18:27,472
[SPEAKER_01]: But let me not develop the view that five precepts will be enough for me to devalue my addiction, because it's not.

237
00:18:29,533 --> 00:18:31,916
[SPEAKER_01]: So if you don't develop the view, then it's fine.

238
00:18:31,936 --> 00:18:34,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you can refine your five precepts onto eight precepts.

239
00:18:35,459 --> 00:18:37,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Then eight precepts to ten precepts, and so on.

240
00:18:38,577 --> 00:18:43,040
[SPEAKER_01]: The common thread to all of that is you wanting to be free from the value of centrality.

241
00:18:44,961 --> 00:18:53,608
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you replace five precepts, if you replace your non-precept with five precepts and say, that's it, see, I have accomplished the restraint, means no.

242
00:18:54,968 --> 00:19:00,973
[SPEAKER_01]: And now you must affect that there is still a lot more to be done in order to uproot the value of centrality.

243
00:19:01,904 --> 00:19:08,445
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you can replace your addiction with God or whatever you want, but don't entertain the notion that you are completely free from addiction.

244
00:19:08,785 --> 00:19:11,266
[SPEAKER_01]: And most people, like on Monday level, they don't do that.

245
00:19:11,366 --> 00:19:16,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Remember, like, you saw that people would say, oh, once an addict, you remain an addict for the rest of your life.

246
00:19:16,667 --> 00:19:19,368
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a question of managing and not returning back to the addiction.

247
00:19:21,048 --> 00:19:25,409
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally means you remain valuing sensuality for the rest of your life.

248
00:19:26,275 --> 00:19:39,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you recognize that particular forms of the essentiality are worse than the other, such as heroin or any other drugs, resulted in far great reaching adverse consequences, but you still value pleasure of senses.

249
00:19:40,742 --> 00:19:45,024
[SPEAKER_01]: And for that, you need more than just the determination of I'll give up this drug or that drug.

250
00:19:45,765 --> 00:19:55,850
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to understand that it has to pertain for the rest of your life, and it has to be seen in its nature as dangerous, not worthy, and that pleasure, it's actually pain.

251
00:19:57,470 --> 00:20:00,271
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you will be able to devalue it.

252
00:20:06,174 --> 00:20:12,137
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's that practice, common practice of a super-practice.

253
00:20:12,458 --> 00:20:14,118
[SPEAKER_00]: It's there for devaluing.

254
00:20:15,199 --> 00:20:15,379
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

255
00:20:15,579 --> 00:20:16,960
[SPEAKER_00]: It's those things that you are attracted to.

256
00:20:17,060 --> 00:20:18,100
[SPEAKER_01]: It can help you initially.

257
00:20:18,141 --> 00:20:19,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

258
00:20:19,421 --> 00:20:24,504
[SPEAKER_01]: People don't see the nature of the value of pleasure, which means they only see the object of their pleasure.

259
00:20:26,574 --> 00:20:30,296
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start practicing sensory strength, you will still value the objects of your pleasure.

260
00:20:30,897 --> 00:20:34,179
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you might want to devalue them a bit by seeing them for what they really are.

261
00:20:34,319 --> 00:20:38,642
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the Subha is, showing you the unattractive basis of everything that you can find attractive.

262
00:20:40,161 --> 00:20:51,846
[SPEAKER_01]: But a subha in itself, exactly, can also become a wrong thing then, because in itself it will not lead you to devalue your dependence on pleasure, which is not in sense objects, nor in your senses.

263
00:20:51,926 --> 00:20:54,427
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in your attitude towards possibility of pleasure.

264
00:20:55,067 --> 00:21:01,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, I renounce these pleasures, because they're not good, but you still value other pleasures that might come along.

265
00:21:02,510 --> 00:21:03,130
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the Buddha means.

266
00:21:03,150 --> 00:21:07,812
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to sit and think about pleasures of heaven and rationalize them.

267
00:21:08,666 --> 00:21:08,766
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

268
00:21:08,966 --> 00:21:22,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Just by implying that there are other pleasures worthy of adhering to, except these ones that you renounced, you establish yourself upon that value, which means it's not... sensuality is not devalued in that universal manner.

269
00:21:23,510 --> 00:21:24,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure of any kind.

270
00:21:24,470 --> 00:21:27,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter if I see things that are agreeable or disagreeable.

271
00:21:27,891 --> 00:21:36,313
[SPEAKER_01]: What matters is pleasure that I get on account of it, and seeking them out on account of wanting that pleasure that comes from them.

272
00:21:37,829 --> 00:21:47,553
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your dependence on your own pleasure of your own senses, it's completely gratuitous and internal, and not to be found in the world.

273
00:21:47,573 --> 00:21:49,174
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just... you have... it's a view.

274
00:21:49,694 --> 00:21:50,694
[SPEAKER_01]: You value the pleasure.

275
00:21:50,814 --> 00:21:52,175
[SPEAKER_01]: You assume them as valuable.

276
00:21:52,835 --> 00:21:54,756
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why we call it one of the upādānas.

277
00:21:55,713 --> 00:21:58,154
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why even a puthujjana can free himself from sensuality.

278
00:21:58,274 --> 00:22:15,023
[SPEAKER_01]: If he were to find a good enough reason to see that any form of sexual activity, any form of sensual pleasure is always unwholesome, always bad, not to be welcomed, not to be engaged with, and stick to it, he would get to see the true nature of it, which is dangerous, not to be welcomed, not to be engaged.

279
00:22:16,103 --> 00:22:19,365
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only reason people engage with sensuality is because they don't see the

280
00:22:20,418 --> 00:22:21,999
[SPEAKER_01]: The danger of it, of that same thing.

281
00:22:22,800 --> 00:22:28,404
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only reason a person enjoys this beautiful beverage, as the Sutta says, is because you don't understand that it's poisonous.

282
00:22:29,485 --> 00:22:32,207
[SPEAKER_01]: If you understand that it's poisonous... By the way, poison is undetectable.

283
00:22:33,669 --> 00:22:39,373
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what's the difference, then, between a poisonous drink, that you don't know it's poisonous, and that you know it's poisonous?

284
00:22:39,473 --> 00:22:41,675
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the knowledge in regard to it that's different.

285
00:22:42,235 --> 00:22:45,298
[SPEAKER_01]: You know that it's poisonous.

286
00:22:45,678 --> 00:22:47,860
[SPEAKER_01]: The pleasure, the taste, has remained perfect.

287
00:22:49,457 --> 00:22:52,258
[SPEAKER_01]: But you now completely don't want it.

288
00:22:52,558 --> 00:22:54,279
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you know it's the poison.

289
00:22:54,339 --> 00:22:55,680
[SPEAKER_01]: That pleasure is the poison.

290
00:22:55,700 --> 00:22:59,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like there is a hidden poison that I can extract from the pleasure and still have the pleasure.

291
00:23:00,002 --> 00:23:01,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure is the poison.

292
00:23:02,743 --> 00:23:03,104
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

293
00:23:03,604 --> 00:23:09,507
[SPEAKER_01]: You regard pleasure as a poison, or as a painful doubt, as the Buddha would say, you will lose any attraction towards it.

294
00:23:10,047 --> 00:23:11,388
[SPEAKER_01]: How long you have to regard it like that?

295
00:23:11,408 --> 00:23:12,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you don't know.

296
00:23:12,208 --> 00:23:13,849
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have to take it in the ultimate sense.

297
00:23:14,624 --> 00:23:17,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, yeah, let me do it for a week and become anāgāmī and then I have to...

298
00:23:17,967 --> 00:23:19,688
[SPEAKER_01]: I can go back to my life or something.

299
00:23:21,609 --> 00:23:21,950
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

300
00:23:23,471 --> 00:23:25,532
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you also get to learn from your sense restraint.

301
00:23:26,093 --> 00:23:29,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Devalue things that need to be devalued, not just blindly adhere to it.

